PATTERN: For |Aut|=1 classes with specific blue counts:
  bl=1: (bk,bl) = (H-1, 1) → bk = H-1, always has bk+1 = H
  bl=3: (bk,bl) = (H-3, 3) → bk = H-3
  bl=5: (bk,bl) = (H-5, 5) → bk = H-5
  bl=7: (bk,bl) = (H-7, 7) → bk = H-7
  bl=9: (bk,bl) = (H-9, 9) → bk = H-9

  FORMULA: For |Aut|=1 classes, (bk, bl) = (H-k, k) where k=blue_count
  This just says black = #tilings - blue = H - blue. (trivially true!)
  The NON-TRIVIAL part: which value k does a given class get?
  k is determined by the class's position in the blue graph.

WHICH H VALUES GET WHICH BLUE COUNTS:

  bl=1 (pendants in blue graph): H=9(n=5), H=15(n=5,|Aut|=3)
  bl=3 (ring/degree-3 in blue): H=5, H=9(n=5), H=11, H=13, H=33, H=45(n=6)
  bl=5 (higher degree): H=17(n=6)
  bl=7: H=29(n=6), H=33(n=6), H=37(n=6)
  bl=9: H=41(n=6), H=45(n=6,|Aut|=3)

  PATTERN: blue count increases with n (the maximum blue grows each n).
  At n=5: max blue = 3. At n=6: max blue = 9.
  The blue counts form ODD NUMBERS: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, ...
  The distribution at each n seems to be: few classes with high blue,
  many with low blue. This mirrors the H distribution (more low-H classes).

THE NON-UNIVERSAL CASES (H,|Aut|) WITH MULTIPLE (bk,bl):

  (9,1): appears as BOTH (6,3) AND (8,1) at n=5.
    These are two DISTINCT iso classes with H=9, |Aut|=1, but different
    grid-sym structure: one has 3 grid-sym tilings, the other has 1.
    At n=5, both are SC. The blue graph topology distinguishes them:
    (6,3) = ring node (more connected), (8,1) = pendant (less connected).

  (45,3): appears as BOTH (6,9) AND (12,3) at n=6.
    Two SC classes with H=45, |Aut|=3, #t=15, but different grid-sym.
    (6,9): 9 grid-sym tilings, HAS a self-loop in blue graph.
    (12,3): 3 grid-sym tilings, no self-loop.

  RULE: When two SC classes have same (H,|Aut|), they get DIFFERENT blue counts
  iff they have different geometric structure in the tiling model.
  The blue count distinguishes them: higher blue = more symmetric class.

THE SELF-LOOP CRITERION:

  A class C has a blue self-loop iff: ∃ T∈C, T grid-sym AND T_flip∈C.
  = ∃ two different tilings T,T_flip in C, both grid-sym, T_flip=tile-complement(T).

  n=4: H=5, blue=3 → 1 self-loop (2 grid-sym tilings within class pair up)
  n=5: NO self-loops
  n=6: H=41 (blue=9) → 1 self-loop, H=45,|Aut|=3 (blue=9) → 1 self-loop

  OBSERVATION: Self-loops appear when blue≥3 AND the class contains
  at least one grid-sym pair (T,T_flip) mapping WITHIN the class.
  At n=5: even though 4 classes have blue=3, NONE have self-loops.
  This is a constraint on the specific tiling geometry, not just the blue count.

THE INTERPLAY OF CONSTRAINTS — UNIQUENESS:

  Given the constraints, can we PREDICT the four-category decomposition?

  KNOWN from theory alone:
  1. total_blue = 2^{free_bits}, total_black = 2^m - 2^{free_bits}
  2. NS pairs have black = 2×H/|Aut| (from iso class spectrum)
  3. H=1,|Aut|=1 class always has (bk,bl)=(0,1) — trivially pure blue

  REQUIRES computation:
  4. Which other SC classes are pure blue (which have all tilings grid-sym)
  5. The blue graph topology (C4-corona at n=5, more complex at n=6)
  6. The blue count per class = position in blue graph

  ONCE KNOWN:
  7. Black per mixed class = #tilings - blue = H/|Aut| - blue
  8. Pure blue sum = Σ_pureBclass blue = sum of small values from highly symmetric classes

  The overlapping constraints narrow the solution space:
  • total_blue must be decomposed into #SC odd parts summing to it
  • #SC odd parts = #SC classes (known)
  • These parts must form a valid degree sequence for the blue graph
  • The blue graph must be connected (observed)
  • The black counterparts = #t - blue must be non-negative even
  These together with the H values of each class pin down all (bk,bl) pairs.
